\details{
These are aliases of the corresponding \link{delay_process} constructors -- a
validation lag is an ordinary non-negative delay, only measured from the
\emph{report} rather than from the event -- so the parameters, priors and
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}} behaviour are identical.  The one
difference is the support, and it depends on the mode: under
\code{retraction_only} the lag lives on \verb{\{1, 2, ...\}} (a retraction lands strictly
after the report it withdraws, so a case retracted in the same period is
dropped by \code{\link[=nowcast]{nowcast()}} -- it was never visible in any data vintage), while
under \code{confirmation_only} and \code{both} it lives on \verb{\{0, 1, ...\}}, since a test
